{"entries":[{"name":"The Seam-Toucher","keys":["Tailor","seam-toucher","seamster","mender"],"content":"The Tailor: cosmic staff of the tapestry-loom, not a god but a mender. For four thousand years has been patching the threadbare weave by harvesting thread from other realities—pulling loose, unfinished stories and braiding them into Magisa's tapestry. Created the summoning holes that bring Chosen to the world. Thirty years ago, botched a pull and leaked the golden thread that became the Drinker. The King is his 'masterpiece,' his finest patch. Pulled Brandon Kelly and Clarissa from a gray intersection—triage, marked them as harvestable spare thread—but a third entity braided them together mid-transit before stitching could occur. Operates from the seams themselves, appears and vanishes between buildings with lagging shadows. Speaks with perfect politeness while discussing the arithmetic of lives. Terrified of the third party now sewn into the capital's court.","caseSensitive":false,"cascade":false,"throttle":100},{"name":"Thornwall","keys":["Thornwall","river town","Guild office"],"content":"Thornwall: a proper river town six days south of Aurelium, where the Coldwater river feeds the mills and no one notices violence done in narrow streets. Home to the Guild's western assessment office on River Street, run by Master Halloway—the one honest assessor, the one the palace never bought. On the nineteenth of Emberfall at midnight, this office became an intersection of cosmic forces: Brandon's threat assessment was forged and re-forged, burned and re-drafted under pressure from Chancellery inspectors seeking a different number. Master Halloway stalled with a faked wrist and blotted cards on purpose, buying time enough for Courier Sable to dispatch a warning. When the Tailor arrived to ensure the forged assessment, time stopped in the street outside. The gap between the buildings flanking River Street—too narrow for a man, wide enough for what departs—still holds a single thread of purple silk.","caseSensitive":false,"cascade":false,"throttle":100},{"name":"Master Halloway","keys":["Halloway","honest assessor","side office"],"content":"Master Halloway: senior assessor of the Thornwall Guild office, a thin gray man with ink-stained fingers, forty years of filing reports the palace stopped reading in '39. On the night of the nineteenth, was forcibly brought to his office by Chancellery inspectors demanding he re-assess Brandon's threat rating—not through violence but through threat of pension loss. Fed twice. Called 'Master' throughout. Watched his original assessment burn in his own stove. Blotted two new cards on purpose, bought time through the precision needed, refused to break. Before the Tailor arrived, had sent Courier Sable racing through the night with a warning parcel, paid from his own purse. Keeps minutes. Some things shouldn't be discoverable by subpoena.","caseSensitive":false,"cascade":false,"throttle":100},{"name":"Courier Sable","keys":["Sable","courier","second class"],"content":"Courier Sable: Courier Second Class of the Thornwall Guild, dispatched by Master Halloway at midnight on his own coin to intercept the Dread Lord's column with a parcel. The parcel bore the Guild's warning: the crystal ball had finished its assessment of Brandon Kelly, output rating SS, and the Chancellery was already pulling traffic records. Rode hard for two days to catch the column at a crossroads on day one. Offered safe passage back. Chose instead to stay—climbed a bad window latch into a riverside office, sat in a room while time was stopped by cosmic forces, stood as witness to a negotiation between a teenager and the staff that patches reality. Now understands that hazard pay was never real, and that the Guild owes her a debt it will never fully pay.","caseSensitive":false,"cascade":false,"throttle":100},{"name":"The Unknown Witness Entity","keys":["third party","unknown watcher","the braider","corner presence"],"content":"The Unknown Witness Entity: a third cosmic force, neither the Tailor nor the loom-keeper, standing on a gray intersection four days before the story began. Observed the Tailor pull Brandon from his crosswalk. Followed the braided thread through the seams without creating a hole of its own. Braided two dead strangers—a courier whose emergency contact was her landlord, and a scheduler who ate lunch alone at his own desk—together mid-transit into the weave. Created a load-bearing thread from two harvested scraps. Has operated in the capital's court for thirty years, woven into the Chancellery's stitchwork so perfectly the Tailor has never once perceived it. Wears a color that godly sight cannot file or perceive. Yminia saw its outline in her vision but looked at the wrong figure first and flinched the memory away. The Tailor fears it more than it fears anything in the weave. Whatever it wants, it chose carefully. It has been waiting a very long time.","caseSensitive":false,"cascade":false,"throttle":100}]}
